TRANSFER(1)                 User Commands                TRANSFER(1)

NAME
    transfer - copy files between minios and the host

SYNOPSIS
    transfer [gui] [HOST[:PORT]]
    transfer serve [-p PORT] [DIR]
    transfer ls [-h HOST] [-p PORT] [PATH]
    transfer get [-h HOST] [-p PORT] [-o DIR] PATH...
    transfer put [-h HOST] [-p PORT] [-d PATH] FILE...
    transfer mkdir|rm [-h HOST] [-p PORT] PATH...
    transfer mv [-h HOST] [-p PORT] FROM TO

DESCRIPTION
    transfer copies files and folders over the protocol MFT 1. The host
    program tools/transfer.py has the same commands and the same window.
    Without a command, or with gui, transfer opens the window. With
    HOST[:PORT] the window connects at once.

    The window has a connection bar, a server bar, the pane "This
    computer" and the pane of the remote folder, the list of transfers
    and a status line. Upload copies the selected local item into the
    remote folder, and Download copies the selected remote item into the
    local folder. A row dragged from one pane to the other is copied as
    well; a drop on a folder row copies into that folder. Files dragged
    from Files onto the remote pane are uploaded. Cancel stops the
    running transfers. Before a copy replaces an item, the window asks.

    HOST defaults to 10.0.2.2, the host in the QEMU user network, and PORT
    to 9103, the port of "tools/transfer.py serve" on the host. serve
    makes DIR (default the current folder) available on PORT, by default
    9102. tools/run.sh forwards host port 9102 to that port, so
    "tools/transfer.py" on the host connects to it.

    A receiver writes each file to a part file named
    .NAME.SIZE-MTIME.mft-part in the target folder and renames it after a
    complete transfer. A cancelled or failed transfer leaves the part
    file, and the next copy of the same file continues from its end.
    Every chunk of data carries a CRC-32 that the receiver checks.

    ls lists a remote folder, get downloads files and folders into DIR
    (default the current folder), put uploads files and folders into the
    remote folder PATH (default the served folder), mkdir creates remote
    folders, rm deletes remote files and folders with their contents, and
    mv renames a remote item. A remote path is relative to the served
    folder.

EXIT STATUS
    0 when every operation succeeded, 1 after a failure, 2 for wrong
    arguments.

SEE ALSO
    xfer(1)
